From: Michele Salerno <mikysal78@gmail.com>
Subject: ARM: dts: armada-385-linksys-rango: wlan LEDs are the wifi PCIe resets

On the WRT3200ACM, gpio1 13 (mpp45) and gpio1 14 (mpp46) are described as
the "wlan_2g" and "wlan_5g" LEDs, but they hold the 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz
88W8964 cards in reset when driven low: with a LED trigger (netdev,
phyXradio) the card disappears from the bus (config space reads
0xffffffff) and mwlwifi logs "adapter does not exist", "buffer is NULL for
tx done ring" and "can't get hw specs" until reboot.

Drop the two LEDs and keep the lines deasserted with a GPIO hog.

See https://github.com/openwrt/openwrt/issues/9517
---
--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/marvell/armada-385-linksys-rango.dts
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/marvell/armada-385-linksys-rango.dts
@@ -63,15 +63,20 @@
 		gpios = <&gpio0 21 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
 		label = "rango:white:sata";
 	};
+};
 
-	led-wlan_2g {
-		gpios = <&gpio1 13 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
-		label = "rango:white:wlan_2g";
-	};
-
-	led-wlan_5g {
-		gpios = <&gpio1 14 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
-		label = "rango:white:wlan_5g";
+/*
+ * mpp45 and mpp46 are not LEDs: they are the PCIe reset lines of the two
+ * 88W8964 cards (mpp45: 2.4 GHz card, mpp46: 5 GHz card). Driving them low
+ * ("LED on") holds the card in reset and the radio stops working. Keep them
+ * deasserted and reserved.
+ */
+&gpio1 {
+	wifi-pcie-reset-hog {
+		gpio-hog;
+		gpios = <13 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>, <14 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+		output-low;
+		line-name = "wifi-pcie-reset";
 	};
 };
 
